<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · site</p><h1 class="enc-title">Baidicheng</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as White Emperor City</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Baidicheng, the &quot;White Emperor City&quot;, is a historic fortress and temple site on a hill above the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yangtze-river">Yangtze</a> at the entrance to the Qutang Gorge, the first of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/three-gorges">Three Gorges</a>, near Fengjie in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chongqing">Chongqing</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>. Tradition links its name to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gongsun-shu">Gongsun Shu</a>, who styled himself the White Emperor in the 1st century CE. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liu-bei">Liu Bei</a>, founder of Shu Han, died there in 223, and Tang poets such as Li Bai and Du Fu celebrated it.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/baidicheng.jpg" alt="Baidicheng" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Yumeto · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:20250523_Baidi_Miao.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
